<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wadai War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1909">1909</a>–<a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai</a> War was the French conquest of the Sultanate of Wadai in present-day eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>. French troops took the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeche">Abéché</a> in 1909 and drove out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Dud Murra, who fought on with support from the neighbouring Masalit sultanate. Despite French defeats at the hands of the Masalit in 1910, Dud Murra surrendered in 1911, and Wadai was absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wadai-war.jpg" alt="Wadai War" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Submission_of_Sultan_Dudmurrah_of_Wadai_(1911).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai</a>, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunjur">Tunjur</a> people in the 16th century, lived on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> herding and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, and its army existed above all to raid for slaves. Allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senussi-order">Senussi order</a>, which controlled trans-Saharan routes, it became the dominant power of the east-central Chad Basin. In 1898–99 the British and French governments agreed to assign Wadai to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> to the British sphere. Dud Murra, or Muhammad Salih, took the throne in 1901 with Senussi backing, and his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-asil">Adam Asil</a>, after a failed coup, found refuge with the French, who meant to make him a puppet ruler.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai</a>'s core was an elite cavalry, 7,000 to 11,000 mounted men by 1900, many of them a warrior nobility, and Dud Murra had built up an arsenal of about 10,000 guns. The French relied on African troops, many of them veterans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabih-az-zubayr">Rabih az-Zubayr</a>'s defeated army. A column of 180 under Captain Jean-Joseph Fiegenschuh occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeche">Abéché</a> in June 1909, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-asil">Adam Asil</a> was installed as kolak, or king.</p><p>Dud Murra fought on as a shadow ruler from the territory of Sultan Taj ad-Din of Dar Masalit, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-dinar">Ali Dinar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a>, who saw the French advance into the disputed borderland as aggression, began to support him. Fiegenschuh's force was wiped out at Wadi Kadja on 4 January 1910. On 8 November 1910 at Dorothe some 5,000 men under Dud Murra and Taj ad-Din destroyed a column of 300 led by the Chad Territory commander Joseph Édouard Maillard; Maillard and Taj ad-Din were both killed. Victor Emmanuel Largeau then ran a counter-insurgency campaign that forced Dud Murra to surrender in October 1911.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Dud Murra was imprisoned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndjamena">Fort-Lamy</a>. A major revolt followed his surrender, and the French ousted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-asil">Adam Asil</a>, suspected of an anti-European conspiracy. Famine and epidemics followed the war, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai</a>'s population fell from 700,000 in 1912 to 400,000 in 1914. In 1917 a French commander ordered the killing of more than 100 Wadaian dignitaries in the Cut-cut massacre, and much of the intellectual elite fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Wadai remained part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French colonial empire</a> until 1960.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wadai War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
